WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
More inside the model.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.933
Eleven more terms: decoding, tokenizers, training tricks, and how big models get split across hardware.

3
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:15.667
Beam search keeps the B best partial sequences, to find a globally high-probability output.

4
00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:21.767
Great for translation, but rarely used for chat, because the text turns bland and repetitive.

5
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.899
Perplexity measures how surprised a language model is by text.

6
00:00:26.899 --> 00:00:29.500
It's the exponential of the average loss.

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.330
Lower means a better fit.

8
00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:36.167
It's the classic metric for base models, and for checking quantization quality.

9
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:40.233
There are three main subword tokenizer families.

10
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:49.300
BPE comes from the GPT lineage, WordPiece from BERT, and SentencePiece is language-agnostic, used by Llama and T5.

11
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.760
All of them split rare words into learnable pieces.

12
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.300
That's the mechanics behind tokenization.

13
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.700
Distillation trains a small student model to mimic a large teacher.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.467
The student matches the teacher's output distributions or reasoning traces.

15
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:11.033
That's how the strongest small models, like Phi, Gemma, and the R1 distills, got that way.

16
00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:18.900
Model merging averages or algebraically combines the weights of several fine-tunes of the same base model.

17
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.548
Methods include SLERP, TIES, and DARE.

18
00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:26.367
You fuse capabilities without any training, and mergekit is the standard tool.

19
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:32.455
LayerNorm normalizes each token's activation vector, to keep training stable.

20
00:01:32.455 --> 00:01:36.200
It's applied before every attention and feed-forward block.

21
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.633
RMSNorm is the cheaper variant that modern LLMs use.

22
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:44.967
Beyond temperature and top-p, there are a few more knobs.

23
00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:48.800
Frequency and presence penalties discourage repetition.

24
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:55.400
Stop sequences end generation at a marker, and logit bias pushes specific tokens up or down.

25
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:01.200
A seed makes sampling reproducible, and max tokens caps output length, and therefore cost.

26
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.567
This is how models too big for one GPU get split.

27
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:10.550
Tensor parallelism slices each layer's matrices across GPUs.

28
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:15.233
Pipeline parallelism assigns layer ranges to different GPUs.

29
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:18.425
Sequence parallelism splits along tokens.

30
00:02:18.425 --> 00:02:22.233
It's the vocabulary of multi-GPU serving and training.

31
00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:29.767
Activation checkpointing says: don't store intermediate activations for the backward pass, recompute them.

32
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:34.447
You trade roughly thirty percent extra compute for huge memory savings.

33
00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:37.100
It's a standard flag in big-model training.

34
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:44.433
When GPU memory runs out, you can park weights or the KV cache in CPU RAM, or on disk.

35
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:47.667
It's slow, but it makes a too-big model runnable at all.

36
00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.444
Accelerate and llama.

37
00:02:49.444 --> 00:02:51.133
cpp do it automatically.

38
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:56.100
In mixture of depths, a router lets easy tokens skip layers.

39
00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.219
Full depth is spent only where it's needed.

40
00:02:59.219 --> 00:03:04.267
It's the depth-wise cousin of MoE, research-tier, so just recognize the name.

41
00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:06.433
Let's recap.

42
00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:09.967
Beam search and perplexity cover decoding and measurement.

43
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.933
Distillation and merging build models cheaply.

44
00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:21.367
Parallelism, checkpointing, and offloading fit big models onto limited GPUs, while tokenizers and norms are the plumbing.
